// Prose for each Learn chapter: how the idea works and one hypothetical worked example.
// Inline links use {source-id|anchor text}; build.mjs resolves them against the source list.
export default {
 1:{"explain":["A forward deployed engineer writes production code inside a customer's problem. The code is the easy part to describe. The hard part is that nobody hands you a ticket. You arrive at a company with a contract, a sponsor who wants results, and a dozen people who each have a different idea of what you are there to do. Before you build anything, you need to know who the customer actually is, which workflow you are changing, and what you are allowed to decide on your own.","Those three answers form a charter. The <em>customer</em> is the group whose daily work should change, which is often different from the person who signed. The <em>sponsor</em> is the person who can unblock access, approve scope, and defend the project when it slips. <em>Decision rights</em> cover what you can change without asking: the schema you write to, the prompts you ship, the users you pilot with. The handoff owner is the person who will run the thing after you leave. If that name is blank on day one, the project will quietly die on the day you move on.","The role pulls in two directions. Ramp describes FDEs who {ramp|cover the full customer lifecycle}, from sales through implementation and long-term support, so context does not get lost between teams. OpenAI's framing of the role {openai-fde|starts from a business outcome} and then asks how to make the solution repeatable. Cognition's Nick Wong describes the job of a special projects engineer as {nick|finding problems and solving them}, which is closer to an internal founder than a consultant. All three share one habit: the engineer owns a result, not a task list.","The common failure is starting with motion. A new FDE wants to show value, so they accept every request, build three half-finished tools, and cannot say at the end which one mattered. The fix is boring: write down one outcome you can measure, record today's number for it, and list what is out of scope. An out-of-scope list feels rude to write. It is the document you will point to in week four when a VP asks for a dashboard.","An experienced FDE decides by asking who will notice if this works. If the answer is a specific team doing a specific job, and you can measure that job today, you have a role. If the answer is \"leadership, eventually,\" keep talking until it is concrete."],"example":["Hypothetical: suppose you join a logistics company's deployment. The contract says \"use AI to improve operations.\" The VP of Operations signed it. In your first week you learn that 12 dispatchers handle carrier exception emails by hand, and each spends about 2 hours a day on them, so the team spends about 24 hours a day in total.","You write a one-page charter. Customer: the dispatch team. Sponsor: the VP, who controls email and TMS access. Outcome: hours spent on exception handling, baseline 24 per day, measured from a two-week time log. Decision rights: you can change routing rules and draft replies, but a dispatcher approves anything sent to a carrier. Handoff owner: the dispatch team lead. Out of scope: billing disputes, the customer portal, and a general analytics dashboard.","The VP pushes back on excluding the dashboard. You agree to revisit it once exception hours drop below 12 per day, which gives the request a clear trigger instead of an open-ended maybe."]},
 2:{"explain":["Customers describe solutions. They rarely describe problems. A support director says \"we need a chatbot\" when the actual issue is that agents spend half an hour hunting for the right refund policy. If you build what was asked, you ship something that works and changes nothing. Discovery exists to find the recurring workflow underneath the request, so that the thing you build attaches to work people already do.","The mechanism is observation over opinion. Ask people about the last specific time the problem happened, not whether they would use a new tool. Past events produce facts: who was involved, what systems they touched, how long it took, what workaround they used. Hypothetical questions produce polite yeses. Y Combinator's talk on {users|how to talk to users} is a fast primer on this style of conversation. Then watch the work itself. Sit next to someone while they do the task, and write down every tab switch, copy-paste, and message to a colleague. Those details are where the time goes.","Ramp's account of its FDE team makes the payoff concrete. When FDEs {ramp|talk to customers directly} and question each requirement, many requests shrink or disappear once the underlying need is clear. Requests that used to travel from an account executive to product engineering often turned into long, delayed projects. The shortcut is not a better spec. It is a shorter path between the engineer and the person with the problem.","The usual mistake is interviewing only the sponsor. Sponsors know the business goal but often do not know how the work is actually done. Another mistake is treating one vivid complaint as a pattern. A problem worth solving shows up in more than one person's week, and it has a cost you can estimate from what they told you, not from what they hope.","An experienced FDE stops discovering when they can draw the current workflow end to end, name the owner of every data source in it, and point to repeated pain with dates attached. Then they show the map to a user and to the sponsor. If both recognize it, you have a problem brief. If only the sponsor recognizes it, you have a strategy slide."],"example":["Hypothetical: suppose an insurance customer asks for \"an AI that summarizes claims.\" You interview three adjusters and ask each about the last claim that took too long. All three describe the same step: matching a repair invoice to the policy's coverage limits, which means opening the policy PDF, a spreadsheet of endorsements, and the claims system side by side.","You sit with one adjuster for a morning. She handles 9 claims and spends about 10 minutes on invoice matching for each, so 90 minutes of a 4-hour morning goes to that one step. Summaries never come up. The written claim notes are already short.","Your problem brief says the pain is coverage lookup during invoice review, not claim summarization. You show the workflow map to the adjuster, who corrects one step, and to the claims VP, who agrees the original request was a guess. The project is now about coverage lookup."]},
 3:{"explain":["After discovery you usually have too many problems, not too few. Scoping is choosing the smallest change that delivers real value and can be tested in a short window. It matters more in field work than in product work because the customer's patience is finite and their trust is earned by the first thing you ship, not the best thing you plan.","Ramp lists {ramp|\"always be scoping\"} among its FDE principles. Their write-up describes a customer blocked by a feature gap estimated at about three engineering days, which the team resolved with a workaround found on a single call with the customer. The same post admits a tension: ad-hoc customizations add complexity, so the team generally prefers general features, and choosing between a quick fix and a reusable feature comes down to judgment. Scoping is how you make that choice on purpose instead of by default.","A useful method is to rank candidates on three axes. Value is how much of the measured problem goes away. Feasibility is whether you have the data, access, and skills today. Uncertainty is how much you would learn by trying. A project with high value and high uncertainty is often the right first bet if you can test it cheaply. A project with high value and low feasibility belongs on a list for later, with the blocker named.","Then write the contract for the pilot. Acceptance criteria say what must be true for the pilot to count as working, in terms a user can check. A baseline says where the number is today. A stop condition says what result would make you quit or change course. The stop condition is the part teams skip, and it is the one that protects you from a pilot that runs for months because nobody wants to call it.","Sierra's interview format rewards the same skill: candidates are judged partly on how they {sierra-interview|fit an idea to a time limit} and cut what does not matter. Experienced FDEs scope by asking what they could show a real user in two weeks that would change a decision. If nothing would change, the scope is wrong, however clean it looks."],"example":["Hypothetical: continuing the insurance case, you have three candidates. A coverage lookup panel in the claims screen scores high on value and high on feasibility, because the policy data is already in one database. Auto-approving small claims scores high on value but low on feasibility, because it needs a compliance review. A claims summary scores low on value, because discovery showed nobody needed it.","You propose a two-week pilot of the lookup panel with 5 adjusters. Baseline: about 10 minutes per invoice match. Acceptance: median time falls to 4 minutes or less on at least 50 matched invoices, with zero coverage errors found in a supervisor spot check. Dependencies: read access to the policy database and one hour of IT time to embed the panel.","Stop condition: if by day 7 the median is still above 8 minutes, you stop building and go back to watching adjusters work. Auto-approval goes on the later list, blocked on compliance."]},
 4:{"explain":["Most FDE projects are data projects in disguise. The model, the interface, and the workflow logic all assume that the right record is reachable, current, and correct. In a real enterprise, records sit in several systems with different owners, refresh schedules, and permission rules. Nabeel Qureshi's {nabeel|reflections on Palantir field work} are a firsthand account of how much of the job this integration work turns out to be.","The useful move is to turn every surprise into an explicit constraint. For each source, write down the schema you depend on, who owns it, how often it refreshes, and what credentials you use to read it. Access boundaries matter as much as columns. If your service account can see records the end user cannot, you have built a permissions leak, even if the code is correct.","Writes need more care than reads. When your system updates another system, the network can fail after the change happens but before you hear back. Stripe's post on {idempotency|idempotency keys} explains the standard answer: the client sends a unique key with each mutating request, and if a retry arrives with the same key, the server returns the saved result instead of doing the work twice. The same post recommends retrying with exponential backoff and random jitter so that many clients do not retry at once. Customer systems often lack this. When they do, you build the deduplication yourself, keyed on something stable like an invoice number.","The common failure is building on a clean extract. An engineer pulls a CSV, builds a demo that works, and only later finds duplicates, missing fields, and records that changed between pulls. Another failure is leaving bad cases undefined. If the data is stale, should the tool refuse, warn, or proceed? If access is denied, does the user see an error or a blank screen? Undefined behavior becomes whatever the code happens to do.","An experienced FDE treats the integration contract as a deliverable. It should let another engineer reproduce your data sample, understand your assumptions, and know what the system does when a source fails. If you cannot write that document, you do not understand the data yet."],"example":["Hypothetical: the coverage lookup panel reads from the policy database, which refreshes nightly, and from an endorsements spreadsheet that underwriters edit by hand. Your first sample of 2,000 policies has 37 duplicate rows from a past migration and 112 policies whose endorsements are missing from the spreadsheet. That leaves 2,000 - 37 = 1,963 unique policies, of which 1,963 - 112 = 1,851 have complete data.","You write the contract. Duplicates are resolved by keeping the row with the latest update timestamp. Policies with missing endorsements show a banner saying coverage limits are incomplete, and the adjuster checks the PDF. If the nightly refresh is more than 36 hours old, the panel shows the data age in red. Access goes through each adjuster's existing claims-system role, never a shared admin account.","Later, adjusters want to flag wrong limits from the panel. Each flag is written with a key built from claim ID and policy ID, so a double click or a retry after a timeout records one flag, not two."]},
 5:{"explain":["A thin vertical slice is one complete user action that runs through every layer of the system: input, data, logic, interface, and result. It is thin because it handles one case, not every case. It is vertical because it touches everything a real version would touch. The point is to find the integration problems, permission gaps, and usability surprises while they are still cheap to fix.","Before building, decide how much of the logic should be model-directed. Anthropic's guide to {agents|building effective agents} draws a clear line. Workflows run language models and tools through predefined code paths. Agents let the model choose its own steps and tool calls. The guide advises starting with the simplest solution and adding complexity only when it clearly improves results, and it notes that agentic systems often trade latency and cost for better task performance. For field work, that means asking three questions in order. Can plain code do this? If not, can a fixed sequence of model calls do it? Only then, does the task truly need the model to plan its own steps?","Most first slices land on the first or second answer. A lookup, a validation, or a routing rule is deterministic. Extracting fields from a messy document is a good fit for a single model call inside a fixed workflow. An open-ended investigation across many tools may need an agent, but you should be able to explain why the fixed version fails before you build the loop.","The usual way this goes wrong is building horizontally. An engineer perfects the ingestion pipeline, then the prompt, then the interface, and discovers in week three that users want the result in a different screen. Another failure is a demo that only works on the happy path, with the limitations unwritten, so the customer assumes it handles cases it does not.","Sierra's interview asks candidates to {sierra-interview|skip boilerplate and demo} a working product, then discuss its path to production. That is the right instinct for a slice too. Ship the one path, put it in front of a real user, change something based on what they do, and write down every assumption you made to get there."],"example":["Hypothetical: the slice for the coverage panel is one action. An adjuster opens a claim with a repair invoice and sees which line items fall within the policy limits. You consider three designs. Plain code can match line items to coverage categories only when the invoice uses standard codes. A fixed workflow, one model call that extracts line items into a typed schema followed by deterministic matching, handles free-text invoices. An agent that browses policy documents on its own adds cost and is hard to test, and nothing in the task needs it.","You build the fixed workflow for auto-body invoices only. The demo uses 20 real invoices: 17 match correctly, 2 have line items the extractor misreads, and 1 uses a coverage category the panel does not know.","The adjuster watching the demo says she needs the remaining limit, not just in or out. You add that field the same day and write down the limitations: auto-body only, a known gap in categories, and extraction errors shown with the original invoice line beside them."]},
 6:{"explain":["A demo proves that a system can work once. A customer needs to know how often it works, how it fails, and whether the failures are tolerable. Evaluation is the step that turns those questions into numbers you can show to the people who will sign off on expansion. Without it, every later conversation about quality becomes an argument between anecdotes: the champion remembers the impressive run, the skeptic the embarrassing one.","Anthropic's {evals|guide to evals for agents} gives a useful vocabulary. A <em>task</em> is one test with defined inputs and success criteria. A <em>trial</em> is one attempt at it, and you run several because outputs vary between runs. A <em>grader</em> scores some part of the result, and the <em>outcome</em> is the real end state, such as whether a record actually exists in the database rather than whether the agent said it created one. The same guide recommends deterministic graders where they work, model graders where judgment is needed, and human review mostly to calibrate the model graders. It also argues that a team can start with a few dozen tasks drawn from real failures instead of waiting to build hundreds.","For FDE work the inputs matter more than the tooling. Representative cases come from the customer's actual queue, not from cases you wrote to show the system off. Edge cases come from the people who handle exceptions today. Unacceptable failures come from the risk owner: the outputs that would end the project if they reached a user, even once. Keep a held-out set that you never tune against, so the final score is not just a measure of how well you memorized the development set.","The common failure is grading the wrong thing. A rigid grader that checks for one exact sequence of steps will reject valid solutions, and a lenient one will pass confident nonsense. The {evals|Anthropic evals article} insists on reading transcripts for this reason: the transcript shows whether a failure belongs to the agent or to the grader. A second failure is skipping the baseline. A new workflow at 85 percent accuracy sounds good until you learn the current manual process is at 92.","An experienced FDE decides expansion on the failure categories, not the headline score. Two systems with the same pass rate can differ completely if one fails on easy, cheap-to-catch cases and the other fails silently on high-value ones. The report should show the categories, the baseline, cost and latency, and the release criteria agreed in advance. The {tax|Thrive and OpenAI tax agent write-up} shows the same habit in a domain where errors are costly: practitioner corrections become test cases, so the evaluation set keeps tracking real work."],"example":["Hypothetical: an insurance claims team wants an assistant that drafts coverage summaries. The FDE pulls 300 closed claims from the last quarter. Two hundred go into a development set, and 100 are locked away as held-out. The claims lead adds a list of unacceptable failures: citing the wrong policy number, or stating that something is covered when the policy excludes it.","On the held-out set the assistant gets 88 summaries fully correct. Of the 12 failures, 9 are formatting or missing-detail problems a reviewer would catch in seconds. Three are wrong coverage statements, which the team had defined as unacceptable. The baseline, junior adjusters drafting by hand, produced 91 correct out of 100, but took about four times longer per claim.","The FDE does not report 88 percent and stop. The report says the system is faster and slightly less accurate overall, and that 3 percent of outputs hit an unacceptable category. The agreed release criterion was zero unacceptable failures on held-out data, so the decision is no expansion yet. The next step is a targeted fix for coverage exclusions plus a mandatory reviewer check on that field, then a rerun on a fresh held-out sample."]},
 7:{"explain":["A pilot that works on a good day is not a production system. Production means the CRM times out, the vendor API returns a 500 halfway through a write, a batch job runs twice, and the person who built the integration is on another project. The FDE's job at this stage is to make partial failure safe and to make the system legible to whoever will run it after you leave. Customers judge you on the bad week far more than on the launch.","Start with timeouts and retries, because they are where well-meant code causes outages. Marc Brooker's {retries|AWS article on retries} explains that retries compound across layers: when every layer of a call stack retries independently, a struggling dependency can see its load multiplied many times over. His advice is to retry at a single point, cap retries, add backoff with jitter so clients do not retry in lockstep, and set timeouts from measured downstream latency percentiles rather than guesses. He also points out that a timeout does not prove a side effect did not happen.","That last point is why writes must be idempotent. Stripe's {idempotency|post on idempotency keys} describes the pattern: the client generates a unique key per operation and sends it with each attempt, and the server stores the result against that key. If the first attempt succeeded but the response was lost, the retry gets the stored result instead of a second charge. In customer integrations the same idea applies to tickets, records, emails, and refunds. Any action you would be embarrassed to perform twice needs a key.","Next, decide what healthy means. The Google SRE chapter on {slo|service level objectives} says to choose indicators from what users care about, keep the number small, and avoid targets of 100 percent because they slow everything down. For an AI workflow, the indicators usually include end-to-end latency, error rate, and some measure of output quality from your evaluation suite. Pair each objective with a containment path: a feature flag, a rollback, or a route to a human queue.","The common failure is treating the runbook as documentation written at the end. A runbook is only real if the future maintainer has used it. Walk them through a simulated outage: how they would notice, what they would turn off, and how they would confirm recovery. If they cannot do it without you, the system is not ready to hand over."],"example":["Suppose an FDE has built an agent that reads support emails and creates refund requests in the customer's billing system. The billing API sometimes takes 20 seconds and occasionally times out. The first version made up to three attempts in the agent, and the HTTP client underneath also made up to three attempts per call, so one slow request could become nine calls to billing.","The FDE removes the client-level retries and keeps one retry layer: at most two retries with jittered backoff, and a timeout set just above the latency observed for nearly all successful calls. Each refund request now carries an idempotency key derived from the email ID and the line item, so a retry after a lost response cannot create a second refund.","The objective agreed with the support lead is that 99 percent of refund requests are created within five minutes of the email arriving. If the error rate on billing calls stays elevated for ten minutes, a flag routes new emails to the human queue. In the walkthrough, the support operations engineer flips that flag, sees the queue absorb the traffic, and confirms that nothing was created twice."]},
 8:{"explain":["A pilot puts the system into real work with real stakes, but under limits you choose. Its purpose is to learn what evaluation cannot show: how people actually use the tool, where it fits awkwardly into their day, and what support it creates. It is also a decision point. A pilot without an owner and an explicit go/no-go review tends to drift into permanent half-adoption, where the system is technically live and nobody is accountable for whether it helps.","The cohort is the first decision. Pick users who represent the eventual population but whose mistakes are recoverable. Document who is eligible, whether anyone needs to consent or be notified, and what data the pilot will touch. Sierra's {ww|WeightWatchers case study} describes this shape: the agent first handled a small share of traffic in the mobile app, on a narrow set of use cases, with a planned pause to evaluate before going further. That team expanded early because results were strong, but the pause was part of the plan from the start.","Then watch real sessions. Training a group and reading the dashboard a week later misses most of what matters. Sit with users, or review recordings if that is permitted, and log every point where someone hesitated, worked around the tool, or asked a colleague. These observations explain the numbers. A low usage rate might mean the tool is bad, or that it lives in a tab nobody opens. Only watching tells you which.","Expert review is the other half. OpenAI's {morgan|Morgan Stanley case study} is about grounding adoption in evaluations run with domain experts, so that users trust the outputs before they rely on them. During a pilot, keep those experts reviewing a sample of real outputs against the thresholds you set during evaluation. Production data drifts from your test set, and the pilot is the cheapest place to discover how far.","The common failure is a pilot that only measures enthusiasm. Champions in the cohort report that they love it, and expansion happens on that basis. An experienced FDE sets the go/no-go criteria before the pilot starts: minimum usage, maximum rate of serious failures, acceptable support load, and the name of the person who will make the call. The review then becomes a comparison against those criteria rather than a negotiation."],"example":["Hypothetical: a logistics company pilots a tool that drafts responses to carrier disputes. The operations director is the named owner. The cohort is 12 of the 60 dispute analysts, chosen across two shifts and three regions. The go/no-go criteria, written before launch, are: at least 60 percent of eligible disputes handled with the tool, a serious-error rate under 2 percent on expert-reviewed samples, and fewer than five support requests per analyst per week by week four.","Over four weeks the cohort handles 1,500 eligible disputes, 1,020 of them with the tool, which is 68 percent. Two senior analysts review 200 sampled drafts and find 3 serious errors, 1.5 percent. Support requests fall from about nine per analyst in week one to three by week four.","Observation finds one problem the numbers hid: night-shift analysts skip the tool for disputes involving partial deliveries, because those drafts are usually wrong. The director decides go, with a condition. Expansion proceeds for all other dispute types, and partial-delivery disputes stay manual until a fix passes evaluation."]},
 9:{"explain":["Adoption and value are what the customer's budget holder will ask about at renewal. Usage alone does not answer that question, and neither does a single before-and-after number. The FDE's task is to connect activity to an outcome the business already cares about, while counting the work the system creates as well as the work it removes.","Start with a funnel that has honest denominators. How many people could use the system, how many did, how many tasks succeeded, and how many users came back. A headline like 500 active users means little until you know whether the eligible population was 600 or 6,000. Repeat use matters more than first use, because first use is often driven by training sessions and curiosity.","Then choose an outcome that sits closer to what the customer is trying to achieve than to what the system does. Sierra's {sonos|Sonos case study} describes measures like time from purchase to first song and a customer effort score that looks across a customer's whole relationship with the company, not just one support contact. The lesson for FDEs is to pick a measure the customer's leadership already tracks, so your result plugs into their existing story about the business.","Value estimates need the full ledger. If a task now takes ten minutes instead of thirty, but every output needs a five-minute review and the team spends hours a week on support and prompt fixes, the net saving is smaller than the headline. Count review time, escalations, maintenance, and the cost of errors that reach customers. Vendor case studies are a useful reference for the kinds of outcomes teams report. The {rakuten|Rakuten case study} reports a large drop in feature delivery time, for example, but these are company-reported figures without published methodology, and your own memo should hold itself to a higher standard.","The common failure is attributing every change to the system. Case mix shifts, seasonal volume changes, a new hire joins, a policy changes. Before claiming a result, check whether the comparison period was similar and whether a group without the tool moved the same way. An experienced FDE writes the impact memo with the denominator, the comparison, and a stated range of uncertainty. A modest claim that survives a finance review builds more trust than a large one that does not."],"example":["Suppose a customer success team of 40 managers gets a tool that drafts account health summaries. After three months, 34 have used it at least once and 26 use it weekly. The team logged 2,400 summaries in the quarter, and 2,000 of those were drafted with the tool.","Before the tool, a summary took about 45 minutes. With it, drafting takes 15 minutes and review adds 10, so each tool-assisted summary saves about 20 minutes. Across 2,000 summaries that is roughly 40,000 minutes, or about 667 hours. The FDE then subtracts about 60 hours of support and prompt maintenance over the quarter, leaving about 607 hours, or roughly 15 hours per manager.","The comparison period, the previous quarter, included a reorganization that slowed everything down, so the FDE also compares against the same quarter a year earlier, which gives a saving about a third smaller. The memo reports a range of roughly 400 to 600 hours, names both comparison periods, and states the open question: whether faster summaries changed any renewal outcome, which will take two more quarters to see."]},
 10:{"explain":["Every deployed system collects complaints, corrections, and workarounds. Most teams treat these as a support queue: fix the individual case, reply to the user, move on. The FDE's goal in this step is to turn each correction into something that lasts, so the same failure cannot come back silently. That is the difference between a system that slowly improves and one that slowly decays as the data and the users change around it.","The first move is classification. When a failure report arrives, decide whether it is a data problem (the source record was wrong or missing), a model problem (the inputs were right and the output was still wrong), an integration problem (a field was mapped wrongly or a call failed), a policy problem (the system followed the rules and the rules were wrong), or a UX problem (the output was right and the user misread it). Each category has a different owner and a different fix. Sending a data problem to prompt tuning wastes a week and leaves the bad record in place.","The second move is to make the failure repeatable. Write it down as an evaluation task with the real inputs and a grader that would have caught it. Anthropic's {evals|article on agent evals} suggests real failures from bug trackers and support queues as a good source of early tasks, and describes how tasks that start as capability tests can graduate into a regression suite once they pass reliably. The {tax|Thrive and OpenAI tax agent write-up} applies the same idea in a professional services setting, where practitioner corrections and production traces feed the evaluation set.","The third move is to ship a fix and check for regressions on held-out cases, not only on the case you fixed. A prompt change that repairs one failure often breaks two others, and you only see that if the old suite runs every time.","The common failure is a loop that is closed in spirit only. Feedback is collected in a form, someone reads it monthly, and there is no link between a report and a change. An experienced FDE insists on traceability: each report has an ID, a category, an owner, an evaluation case, and a release where the fix shipped. When the customer asks whether their complaint mattered, you can show them the chain. That record is also what lets the customer's own team keep the loop running after you leave."],"example":["Hypothetical: a procurement team uses an assistant that extracts payment terms from supplier contracts. In one month, users file 18 correction reports. The FDE classifies them: 7 are data problems, scanned contracts with unreadable pages; 5 are model problems, where net-60 terms buried in an appendix were missed; 3 are integration problems, a currency field mapped to the wrong column; 2 are UX problems, users confusing the invoice date with the due date; and 1 is a policy question for legal. That accounts for all 18.","The FDE turns three of the appendix cases into evaluation tasks with graders that check the extracted payment term. The current system fails all three. A change to the extraction prompt, which now searches appendices explicitly, makes all three pass.","Before release, the FDE runs the full held-out set of 150 contracts. The old version scored 138 correct. The new version scores 141, and no previously correct contract now fails. The fix ships, the three cases join the regression suite, and each of the five model-problem reports is linked to the release that addressed it."]},
 11:{"explain":["A first deployment proves the workflow can work for one customer. It does not prove the team can afford to do it again. Most first deployments are full of choices made under deadline: a field mapping hardcoded for one CRM, a prompt that names one customer's product lines, a scheduled job that only the FDE knows how to restart. This step turns that work into something a second customer, and a second engineer, can use without you in the room.","The mechanism is a clean split into three layers. <strong>Configuration</strong> holds values that differ per customer: field names, thresholds, policy text, credentials. <strong>Reusable components</strong> hold the parts that should not change: the connector, the evaluation harness, retry and logging logic. <strong>Product gaps</strong> are the things you built around because the core product lacked them. A simple test tells you whether the split is right. If onboarding a new customer requires a code change, a line is in the wrong place. {ramp|Ramp's FDE write-up} favors general features over one-off customizations because those customizations make the codebase harder to maintain, and it admits that knowing when to generalize takes judgment, since it pulls against shipping a small scope quickly. {openai-fde|OpenAI's account of FDE work} frames the same goal: start from a business outcome, then make the solution repeatable.","There are two common ways to get this wrong. The first is generalizing too early: you build a platform from a single example and guess the wrong axes of variation. The second is never generalizing: every deployment becomes a fork, the handoff is a chat thread, and the original FDE stays on call forever. A practical rule is to parameterize only along axes you have seen vary at least twice.","Product feedback follows the same discipline. A note that says one customer wants a feature is easy to ignore. A memo that shows the same workaround at two customers, the hours each one cost, what breaks without it, and what building it would trade off is hard to ignore. {nick|Nick Wong's Cognition profile} shows the upside: an internal security problem nobody owned became the starting point for a security product.","The handoff is finished when another engineer can operate the workflow using only what you wrote, and when someone is named as its owner. Then measure the second deployment in engineer-hours and compare it with the first. That number, more than any architecture diagram, shows whether you generalized the right parts."],"example":["Hypothetical: you built an agent that drafts renewal-risk summaries for a SaaS customer's account managers. The first deployment took 120 engineer-hours: 45 on the CRM connector and evaluation harness, 30 on customer-specific field mapping and prompt policy, 25 on debugging, and 20 on custom alert routing.","Before the second customer, you move the field mapping and policy text into a configuration file and keep the connector and harness as shared code. You estimate the second deployment at 30 hours: 12 for configuration, 10 for a new labeled evaluation set, 8 for alert routing. A colleague who has never seen the project runs it from your handoff document. They get stuck twice, once on an undocumented secret rotation step and once on an unexplained risk threshold. You fix both gaps in the document. The measured second deployment comes in at 34 hours, against 120 for the first.","Alert routing was custom work at both customers, 20 hours and then 8. That repeated cost goes into the product feedback memo as evidence for a shared notification feature, with the tradeoff stated plainly."]},
 12:{"explain":["The last step is being able to explain what you built, why you chose it, and what you still do not know. Interviews and internal reviews for FDE work test the same thing: judgment under ambiguity. Code volume matters much less. A reviewer wants to see that you understood the customer's problem, considered real alternatives, measured the result, and can name the next decision.","Employers now design interviews around this. {sierra-interview|Sierra's AI-native interview} has candidates plan a product idea, build it for about two hours with AI tools, and then review the demo, the code, and what it would take to ship. Interviewers look at how the candidate scoped the work, whether they changed direction when stuck, and the quality of the data model. Candidate reports such as {palantir-loop|one Palantir FDSE loop account} describe rounds on learning, problem decomposition, and past projects. A single uncorroborated report is a useful practice prompt, not a description of every loop.","A strong presentation follows a fixed order: problem, alternatives, design, results, next decision. Each claim should point to an artifact from earlier work: the evaluation report, the before and after measurement, the handoff test. The common failure is a demo-only story. The demo shows the happy path, the numbers have no denominator, and failures are left out. Experienced reviewers read that as a lack of judgment, because every real deployment has failure slices. Naming yours, with the reason and the fix, is the strongest signal you can give. The same applies to limits you never tested: say which customer conditions the results do not cover, and what evidence would change your recommendation.","Self-assessment works the same way. For each skill, such as discovery, integration, evaluation, or handoff, point to the evidence. Where the evidence is thin, say so. Then choose the next capability to improve by comparing your weakest evidence with the work you want to do next. An engineer heading toward agent deployments with no evaluation artifact should fix that before polishing anything else."],"example":["Suppose your project is a renewal-risk summary agent. You open with the problem: account managers spent about 40 minutes preparing each renewal review. You list the alternatives you rejected: a dashboard alone, which showed the data but not the reasons, and a fine-tuned model, which needed labels you did not have. Then you show results. On 200 labeled accounts, two account managers rated 170 summaries usable, or 85 percent. Of the 30 that failed, 12 contained a wrong contract value. In a 25-account pilot, preparation time fell to about 15 minutes.","The interviewer asks about the 12 errors. You have already sliced them: all 12 came from accounts with contracts in more than one currency. Your next decision is to exclude those accounts until currency normalization ships, and you say who owns that change.","Your self-score follows the same pattern. Evaluation is strong, backed by the labeled set. Discovery is weak: you interviewed only three users. That becomes the next capability you work on."]},
 13:{"explain":["An average success rate compresses many different tasks into one number. A model change can raise that number while making the rare, costly failures worse, because the gains come from common easy tasks and the losses come from uncommon severe ones. Users notice the severe failures first. This field note is about keeping those failures visible when the headline score improves.","Three separate things need checking. First, what the agent said. Second, what state it left behind in the customer's systems. Third, whether your grader judged either one correctly. {evals|Anthropic's guide to agent evals} defines the outcome as the final state of the environment, such as whether a reservation actually exists in the database, rather than whether the agent claims success. It also separates capability evals, which start with low pass rates, from regression evals, which should sit near 100 percent so that backsliding shows up immediately. A release that passes on the representative set but drops on the regression suite has told you something the average hid.","The grader needs its own validation. {hamel|Hamel Husain and Shreya Shankar's evals FAQ} recommends starting with error analysis on real traces, so you measure failures you have observed instead of ones you imagined. It recommends checking an LLM judge against expert labels and tracking two rates: how many real failures it catches and how many good outputs it correctly passes. It also warns that repeatedly tuning against a development set overfits the judge, so a test set should stay untouched until the final check. Without this, a judge that reads only the reply text can pass an agent that took the wrong action.","An experienced FDE keeps two kinds of evidence side by side. One is a representative sample that estimates normal performance. The other is a deliberately difficult regression suite built from past incidents and high-severity tasks. False acceptance of harmful outcomes gets its own row in the release table, with a sample size and a named decision owner. A candidate that improves the average and worsens the severe slice does not ship until someone with authority accepts that trade in writing."],"example":["Hypothetical: a support agent can issue refunds. On 1,000 representative tasks, the baseline passes 820 and the candidate passes 860, an improvement from 82 to 86 percent. The team is ready to release.","You slice by severity. Among 40 refund tasks in the regression suite, the baseline issued a wrong refund in 2 cases and the candidate in 6. You then check the grader on 100 expert-labeled cases: 30 true failures and 70 good outputs. It catches 24 of the 30 failures, an 80 percent catch rate, and passes 66 of the 70 good outputs. Looking at the 6 wrong refunds, you find the grader marked 4 of them as passes. It read the polite reply and never checked the payment ledger.","The decision is to block the release. You add a state check that compares the ledger with the expected refund, report harmful false accepts as a separate line, and rerun both suites before the next review."]},
 14:{"explain":["Single sign-on tells a system who the user is. Authorization decides what that user may read or change right now. Retrieval systems make the second question harder because they copy data into indexes, caches, previews, and generated summaries, and each copy can carry an old view of permissions. Removing a user's access is therefore a consistency problem: the permission change and the content change have to be applied in the right order everywhere the content lives.","{zanzibar|Google's Zanzibar paper} calls this the new enemy problem. In one of its examples, Alice removes Bob from a document and then new content is added. Bob should not see the new content, but he can if the access check uses a stale permission list from before his removal. Zanzibar addresses it with a consistency token stored alongside each content version, so later checks are evaluated against permissions at least as fresh as that content. Most teams do not need Zanzibar itself. They need the same invariant: the authorization snapshot used for a read must be no older than the content version being returned.","Two further rules apply at every boundary. {authorization|OWASP's authorization cheat sheet} recommends denying by default, checking permissions on every request rather than most of them, and writing tests for authorization logic. When an agent reaches data through a tool server, {mcp|MCP security guidance} says servers must not accept tokens that were not issued to them, and it forbids passing a client's token straight through to downstream APIs. A token's audience defines where its trust ends.","The common mistake is testing only the first retrieval. Initial search results are usually checked. Cached results, citation previews, exports, and summaries generated before the revocation often are not. An experienced FDE writes down the revocation behavior the customer can rely on, including how quickly it takes effect and which conditions cause the system to fail closed. Then they test it as a sequence across users and tenants, because a single request cannot show an ordering bug."],"example":["Hypothetical: an enterprise search agent serves two tenants. The index refreshes content every 2 minutes but refreshes permissions every 30 minutes. Bob is removed from a matter folder at 10:00. Alice edits a memo in that folder at 10:02, and the new version is indexed at 10:04. At 10:05 Bob searches. The index holds the new memo with a permission snapshot taken at 9:45, which still lists Bob. He receives a snippet of content written after he lost access.","The fix has three parts. Retrieval checks the authoritative permission service on every request. Cached results are partitioned by user and tenant. If the permission snapshot is older than the content version, the result is withheld, so the system fails closed.","The regression suite replays grant, fetch, revoke, edit, fetch for two users in each of two tenants, across three surfaces: search results, citation previews, and export. That is 2 users times 2 tenants times 3 surfaces, or 12 checks per run."]},
 15:{"explain":["When a call to another system times out, three different things may have happened. The request never arrived. The request arrived and failed. Or the request succeeded and only the response was lost. The caller cannot tell these apart from the timeout alone. An agent that treats every timeout as a failure and retries will sometimes apply the same change twice.","{idempotency|Stripe's post on idempotency} describes exactly these cases and points out that the outcome can be ambiguous from the client's side. Its answer is an idempotency key: the client attaches a unique ID to the operation and reuses it on every retry, so the server can recognize the repeat and return the saved result instead of acting again. {retries|Marc Brooker's AWS article on retries} adds the operational rules. A timeout does not prove failure, so retry operations with side effects only when they are idempotent. Retry at one layer of the call stack, because stacked retries multiply load; its example reaches a 243-fold increase on a struggling database. Space retries with capped exponential backoff and jitter.","Agent frameworks add a subtle trap. {twelve|12-Factor Agents factor 5} argues for deriving execution state, such as the current step and retry counts, from the agent's recorded history, which makes a paused task easy to resume. That helps with resumption. It does nothing for the external system. Persisting the conversation or the retry counter does not make the CRM write happen exactly once.","Many enterprise APIs do not support idempotency keys, so an FDE has to build the contract from what exists. Look for a durable operation identifier, such as an external ID field you can write and later query. Find the commit boundary and a status lookup. Then choose explicitly between deduplication, reconciliation after the fact, and compensation. Keep all retries inside the overall deadline. When completion cannot be established safely, report the outcome as unknown and tell the user what to check. Reporting a confident failure for a write that succeeded is worse."],"example":["Suppose an agent updates an opportunity's stage and amount in a CRM and also appends an activity note. The overall deadline is 30 seconds. Each attempt times out at 8 seconds, with up to three attempts and backoff waits of 1 and 2 seconds. The worst case is 8 + 1 + 8 + 2 + 8, or 27 seconds, inside the deadline.","On the first attempt the CRM commits at 7.5 seconds, but the response is lost and the client times out at 8. Setting the stage and amount again is harmless, because those writes set fixed values. The note is an append, so a naive retry creates a duplicate.","You change the design. The agent writes its operation ID into an external ID field on the note. Before retrying, it queries for that ID. If the note exists, the operation is recorded as committed. If the lookup also fails within the remaining budget, the state is recorded as unknown, and the user is told the update may have been applied and which record to check."]},
 16:{"explain":["A retrieval system answers in two halves. The first half decides which evidence reaches the model. The second half turns that evidence into an answer. When a customer reports that the assistant quoted the wrong policy, the instinct is to blame the second half, because that is the part people can read. A stronger model, a longer prompt, or a more elaborate agent loop then gets proposed. Often none of these touch the actual fault, because the correct passage never arrived.","Treat the pipeline as a chain of stages and find the earliest one that failed. Is the right document in the index at all? Is the user allowed to see it, and did an access filter remove it? Is the indexed copy current? Did chunking cut the sentence that matters away from the heading that gives it meaning? Did the correct chunk appear among the candidates, and did reranking keep it near the top? Only after all of that does the question become whether the model used the evidence it was given. Each stage can be checked with a question and an expected source passage, without involving generation at all.","Exact identifiers deserve their own test set. A policy number, a SKU, or a contract clause ID behaves very differently from a broad question like \"what is our refund stance for enterprise customers\". Semantic search is often weak on the first kind and lexical search on the second. Running a simple lexical or hybrid baseline next to the current setup tells you which kind of query is failing and gives you a floor that any fancier design has to beat.","The common failure is optimizing what is easy to see. Better prompts make answers more fluent and more confident, which can hide that the evidence was missing. Anthropic's {evals|guide to agent evals} makes a related point about scores in general: read transcripts before trusting a number, and suspect the task or grader when everything fails. The same discipline applies here. A failure ledger that maps each bad answer to its first broken stage turns an argument about models into a list of fixes with owners. {morgan|Morgan Stanley's evaluation account} is a useful reference for treating retrieval quality as something experts assess explicitly.","An experienced FDE decides by counting. If most failures sit in coverage or permissions, the work belongs to data owners and integration, not to the model. If they sit in ranking, a reranker or hybrid search is the next experiment. Changing the model comes last, and only when the right passage was present and still ignored."],"example":["Hypothetical: an insurer's internal assistant gives wrong answers about claim limits. The FDE builds a set of 200 questions, each with the passage that should support the answer, and collects 50 answers that reviewers marked wrong.","Walking each of the 50 through the chain gives a ledger. In 12, the governing policy was never indexed because it lived in a shared drive the connector skipped. In 7, an access filter removed the document for the asking role. In 9, chunking separated a limit table from its heading. In 18, the right chunk was retrieved but ranked below the cutoff. Only 4 had the correct passage in context and still produced a wrong answer. That is 50 in total, and 46 of them failed before the model was involved.","The plan follows the counts. The connector gap and the permission rule go to the data and identity owners. Chunking keeps tables with their headings. A hybrid search baseline is tested on the 18 ranking failures, with exact policy IDs measured separately. The model upgrade the sponsor asked about is deferred, since at most 4 of the 50 failures could have been fixed by it."]},
 17:{"explain":["Price per token is the number on the vendor's pricing page, so it is the number that gets compared. The customer does not pay for tokens, though. They pay for completed work, and a task that fails, gets retried, or needs a person to fix it costs far more than its inference bill. The FDE's job is to change the denominator from tokens or calls to accepted task outcomes.","The mechanism is simple arithmetic that is easy to skip. Put every cost of producing outcomes in the numerator: inference for all attempts including failed ones, retrieval and infrastructure, human review, rework, support tickets, and an amortized share of implementation. Divide by the number of outputs that were actually accepted. A cheaper model with a lower success rate spreads its costs over fewer accepted tasks and usually adds review time to each one. Review time is often the largest line, which is why it has to be measured rather than assumed.","Consistency matters as much as average quality. Anthropic's {evals|guide to agent evals} distinguishes between succeeding at least once in k tries and succeeding every time, and shows how a 75% per-trial rate becomes roughly 42% when three trials in a row must pass. For a customer-facing workflow, the second number is closer to what operators experience. It also notes that human grading is the slowest and most expensive kind, which is the same cost that shows up as review minutes in production.","The usual mistake is in the other direction. A team multiplies minutes saved by a salary rate and reports a savings figure. Minutes released are capacity, not cash. They become savings only if the customer reduces spend, and they become value only if people move the time to useful work. Keep three lines separate: cash saved, capacity released, and capacity actually redeployed.","An experienced FDE compares options under the same quality bar and workload, then shows a sensitivity table instead of a single ROI figure. Volume, success rate, review minutes, and support hours each get a range. If the conclusion flips inside a plausible range, that is the finding. {openai-fde|OpenAI's description of FDE work} frames the role around business outcomes, and cost per accepted outcome is how that framing turns into a budget decision."],"example":["Suppose a team compares two models for drafting supplier dispute responses, using 1,000 attempts each and an illustrative loaded review cost of $1 per minute. Model A costs $0.40 per attempt, 90% of drafts are accepted, and review takes 4 minutes each. Model B costs $0.10 per attempt, 60% are accepted, and review takes 9 minutes each.","Model A: $400 inference plus 4,000 review minutes is $4,400, spread over 900 accepted drafts, about $4.89 each. Model B: $100 inference plus 9,000 review minutes is $9,100, over 600 accepted drafts, about $15.17 each. On inference alone, B looks cheaper per accepted draft ($0.17 against $0.44), which is exactly the comparison that misleads.","The FDE does not report \"A saves $10 per task\" as cash. The review minutes are capacity, so the write-up states that A releases about 5,000 reviewer minutes per 1,000 attempts and asks the operations lead what that time will be used for. The table also shows that if B's acceptance rose to 85% with 5-minute reviews, the gap would narrow sharply."]},
 18:{"explain":["Pilots are usually judged by the people who sponsored them, in the meeting where the demo runs. Adoption is decided later, by operators doing the real task under time pressure, often with nobody from the project watching. A system can pass the first test and fail the second without any technical defect. Operators go back to spreadsheets because the new path adds steps, hides exceptions, shifts review work onto them, or simply is not what they are measured on.","Three different events get confused. A sign-off means the sponsor liked what they saw. A login means someone opened the tool. A successfully completed task means the system did real work that the operator accepted. Only the last one says anything about value, and only repeat successful use says the workflow has changed. Count them separately, starting from the users who were actually eligible for the pilot rather than everyone who received an invitation.","The mechanism behind abandonment is usually outside the software. Watch the operator do the task. Look at handoffs to other teams, how exceptions are handled, and who has to review the output. Ask who owns the policy or data when something changes. Training is part of the deliverable too. Sierra's account of {university|its customer training program} describes cohorts that mix customer experience, operations, and engineering staff, on the reasoning that the people who know the business should be able to shape the agent. Operators who cannot diagnose a failure tend to route around it.","Scope control helps you see the real pattern before it spreads. In {ww|the WeightWatchers case study}, the first test was a small share of mobile app traffic and a few use cases, with expansion decided after that test. That kind of start keeps the eligible cohort small enough to observe directly.","An experienced FDE expands only after repeat success in an eligible cohort, with a named operator who owns the workflow and a clear support path. If the system creates hidden work somewhere else, such as extra checks in another team, the FDE treats that as a workflow defect and revisits the design instead of pushing for wider rollout."],"example":["Hypothetical: a logistics customer pilots an assistant that drafts carrier exception notes. The sponsor signed off after the demo. Six weeks later, the FDE builds a cohort table for the 40 dispatchers who handle exceptions and were eligible.","Of the 40, 34 logged in, 22 completed at least one accepted note, and 9 were still completing accepted notes in week six. Sitting with dispatchers explains the drop from 22 to 9. Notes for international shipments required customs codes the system did not fetch, so dispatchers copied them in by hand, and the supervisor checked every note because no one had agreed a review rule.","The FDE does not propose a wider rollout. The next step is to add the customs lookup, agree a sampling rule for supervisor review with the operations lead, name a dispatcher as the workflow owner, and rerun the same cohort table for four weeks. Expansion waits for repeat use to hold."]},
 19:{"explain":["Field work produces custom code by design. An FDE solves one customer's problem in that customer's environment, and the fastest path is usually a branch. The trouble starts on the third similar request. Three branches that solve nearly the same problem are three things to upgrade, debug, and explain, and each one depends on whoever wrote it.","The decision is when to turn repeated field work into a supported capability. Start by separating the work into four parts: behavior that is truly shared, configuration that varies by customer, domain policy that belongs to the customer, and work that is genuinely unique. Only the first part is a product candidate. The second part becomes its interface. The third stays with the customer as configuration or rules they own. The fourth stays custom and is labeled as such.","Counting reuse is not enough. Code reuse at the first deployment is cheap to show. What decides whether a package is worth keeping is the cost after that: how long upgrades take across all instances, how many support exceptions each one generates, and whether anyone other than the original author can run it. A capability earns promotion when repeated evidence justifies the maintenance contract that comes with it. That contract includes an owner, versioning, evaluation cases that travel with the package, and an upgrade path.","Ramp's write-up on {ramp|forward deployed engineering} describes this tension directly. Its team weighs a quick custom fix against a reusable feature, treats ad hoc customer customizations as a cost to maintainability, and credits judgment for choosing between scoping small and building general. {openai-fde|OpenAI's account of the role} also stresses making a solution repeatable once the outcome is proven.","The common failure is mistaking a copy for a product. Forking a repository for each customer creates another instance with another set of drift. An experienced FDE tests productization with a second engineer deploying to a second environment from documentation alone, and measures effort, exceptions, and how often they had to ask the original author. That measurement is the evidence that the capability can live without its creator."],"example":["Hypothetical: an FDE team has built ticket-sync integrations for three customers on three branches. Each pulls tickets from a helpdesk, maps fields, and writes summaries to a CRM. Separating the code shows that authentication, pagination, retry handling, and summary formatting are the same everywhere. Field mappings differ by customer. Escalation rules are each customer's own policy. One customer needs a legacy on-premises export, which stays custom.","The team packages the shared parts with a mapping file, a policy hook, versioning, and twelve evaluation cases. A second engineer who did not write any branch then deploys it for a fourth customer using only the docs. It takes 5 working days, against 2 for the original author on earlier deployments. They hit 6 exceptions, and 4 of those required asking the original author.","Those 4 questions become the next documentation and configuration changes. The team repeats the second-engineer test and treats one with no author questions as the bar for calling the package supported."]},
 20:{"explain":["Recovery work is usually measured by time: how fast the service came back. That is the number on the dashboard and in the incident timeline. For systems that hold customer data, time is the second question. The first is whether the data that the system accepted is still correct after recovery. Automated failover can restore connectivity in seconds and still leave writes that disagree between the old primary and the new one.","The mechanism is ambiguity at the moment of failure. When a primary fails, some writes have been acknowledged to clients but not yet replicated. Some requests timed out with an unknown outcome. Background consumers may have processed messages against state that is about to be rolled back. AWS's guidance on {retries|timeouts and retries} points out that a timeout does not confirm an operation failed, and Stripe's post on {idempotency|idempotency keys} explains why retries are only safe when the operation is designed to apply once. During failover, both problems arrive together.","So the FDE names the invariant first. It might be that no payment is applied twice, that an inventory count never goes negative, or that an approved record is never silently lost. Then the team decides in advance when to stop accepting writes, pause queue consumers, serve degraded read-only results, or reconcile, and who makes that call. These choices trade availability for correctness, and they are much easier to make before an incident than during one.","The common mistake is trusting infrastructure health. A green database, healthy replicas, and normal latency say nothing about whether accepted writes survived. The Google SRE chapter on {slo|service level objectives} notes that correctness is often a property of the data rather than the infrastructure, which is why it needs its own check.","An experienced FDE exercises the full recovery, not just the failover trigger. A drill restores from backup, replays the backlog, and records the protected invariant, the data-loss exposure, the time to restore, and the conditions under which replay is safe. The drill result, not the failover time, tells the customer what they can rely on."],"example":["Suppose a customer's order service writes about 150 orders per second to a primary database with asynchronous replication, and replica lag during peak runs near 4 seconds. If the primary fails at peak, roughly 600 acknowledged orders may exist only on the old primary. Automated failover promotes the replica in about 30 seconds and the dashboard turns green.","The FDE names the invariant: every acknowledged order is fulfilled exactly once. Given that, the agreed runbook pauses the fulfillment consumer at failover, so the warehouse does not act on state that may be incomplete. The checkout path keeps accepting new orders with idempotency keys, so client retries cannot create duplicates.","Once the old primary is reachable, a reconciliation job compares its order IDs with the new primary's and replays the missing ones, which the keys make safe. Only then is the consumer resumed. The drill records 30 seconds to restore connectivity, about 600 orders of exposure, and the extra minutes the reconciliation took. That last number is the one the customer cares about."]}
};
